Minor friction burn from treadmill belt slip to knee
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Superficial raw epidermal loss, Intense air-exposed stinging, High-velocity synthetic surface friction mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-015
Reason for Visit: Acute painful skin loss and bleeding following a high-speed fall, requiring assessment for deep tissue damage and wound debridement.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage described a high-velocity friction mechanism resulting in intense pain and skin loss. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to perform critical wound decontamination, exclude full-thickness dermal necrosis or joint penetration, and apply specialized barrier dressings before daytime clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I slipped on the treadmill belt while it was running, and it completely ripped the skin off my knee.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a high-speed sliding mechanism against a rough synthetic surface occurring 1 hour ago. This resulted in a localized superficial epidermal abrasion. The patient notes intense, sharp stinging and burning pain that is severely exacerbated by air exposure or light touch. Small pinpoint areas of bleeding were present initially but have slowed. There is no deep tissue pain.
Functional Status: Fully independent, walking with a stiff-legged antalgic gait to avoid stretching the injured skin.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor hindrance in executing full active range of motion tasks temporarily.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/84 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, protecting the affected knee, keeping the leg strictly extended to minimize pain.
Physical Exam: Dermatological assessment reveals a localized superficial epidermal abrasion with a raw, red gravel rash appearance, confirming superficial raw epidermal loss. The depth is shallow, confined entirely to the epidermis and superficial dermis, lacking deep tissue or fat exposure. Small pinpoint areas of punctate capillary bleeding and serous exudate are present without massive hemorrhage. An erythematous margin is strictly confined to the immediate border of the mechanical friction. Complete absence of full-thickness skin loss, eschar formation, or leathery white tissue. Normal distal neurovascular examination of the affected limb.
Environmental Observations: Treadmill located in the living room; belt appears intact but abrasive.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Minor friction burn (superficial partial-thickness) to knee - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Full-thickness burn - Ruled out due to: Absence of white, leathery, painless, or charred deep tissue.

Open joint laceration - Ruled out due to: Intact joint capsule with damage isolated strictly to the superficial dermis.

Cellulitis - Ruled out due to: Immediate acute traumatic mechanism with erythema purely localized to the direct friction borders.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of a high-velocity friction burn required immediate assessment to rule out deep tissue involvement, embedded foreign material, and joint space penetration. Bedside evaluation confirmed a superficial partial-thickness abrasion without joint compromise. The urgent after-hours evaluation was critical to safely perform necessary saline debridement of synthetic particulate matter and apply immediate occlusive hydrocolloid dressings, effectively terminating the severe neuropathic pain caused by air exposure, which could not be delayed until the next morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed gentle mechanical debridement with sterile saline. Applied a non-adherent silicone/hydrocolloid dressing to seal the wound bed from air exposure.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the sealed dressing securely in place for 3 to 5 days without peeling it back to "check" the wound. Maintaining this undisturbed moist environment dramatically accelerates epithelial cell migration and minimizes painful scar formation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Hydrocolloid or silicone foam dressings.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 3 days for a scheduled dressing change and wound inspection.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department if the wound begins discharging thick, foul-smelling yellow pus, if expanding redness travels up or down the leg, or if a systemic fever develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Friction burns can trap microscopic debris; monitor closely for delayed signs of infection.
Clinical Course: The intense stinging will resolve within 24 hours under the dressing. A thick, yellowish exudate is normal under hydrocolloid dressings and is not an infection. Complete healing takes 7 to 14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the area around the burn becomes hot, swollen, and increasingly red over the next few days.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The occlusive dressing acts as artificial skin, protecting the exposed, highly sensitive nerve endings from moving air, which immediately stops the intense stinging pain.
Actionable Steps: Do not let the dressing get soaked in the shower. Do not rip the dressing off. Expect the dressing to look "bubbly" as it absorbs fluid; this is normal.

